    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A Saharan kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#libya">Libya</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fezzan">Fezzan</a>, sustained by underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#foggara">foggara</a> channels tapping fossil water beneath the desert.</li><li>It controlled trans-Saharan routes, sending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a>, enslaved people and wild animals to Carthaginian and Roman markets.</li><li>As the water table sank below the channels and trade faltered, it faded into scattered oases before the Arab conquest.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wadi-al-ajal">Wadi al-Ajal</a> alone the Garamantes dug an estimated 550 or more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#foggara">foggara</a> channels with over 100,000 vertical shafts to bring water to their fields.</p></aside>
    </section>

